## How SECRET can reach println (threat model)

Three rails exist. They are **not** equivalent.

1. **Check / LLVM / C front-door** (`check_secret_println_sinks`
   L175–193): on IDENT that resolves to `println`/`eprintln`, skip at
   most **one** `LPAREN`, then walk tokens until the **first** `RPAREN`.
   Refuse IDENT via `c_secret_refuse_sink_resolved`. **No paren depth.**
2. **Stmt emit belt** (`c_emit_println`): per-arg IDENT `__sec__` check,
   then `c_emit_expr_c` (depth-correct), then next comma-arg. **Sees
   later args.** Does **not** call `c_secret_emit_guard`.
3. **Free-call emit-guard** (`c_secret_emit_guard` →
   `c_guard_scan_idents`): C-text ident scan of the **full** `args`
   string. Parens are just punctuation. This is the 2.9 target rail.

FN classes:

| ID | Shape | Why FN |
|----|-------|--------|
| F1 | `println((n), tok)` / `println(id(1), tok)` | Walker `RPAREN` closes at the group/call; later SECRET never seen |
| F2 | Same on `eprintln` | Same check walker; LLVM aliases eprintln→println builtin |
| F3 | `oodac emit-bc` any `println(tok)` | **No** `check_secret_println_sinks` in `bc_emit*` → `CALL println` |
| F4 | LLVM builtin `print(tok)` | `print` is a println **shape** (`llvm_emit_expr.oo` L132) and is **not** a check sink |
| F5 | User `fn leak(x) { println(x); } leak(tok)` | Formal `x` is not the SECRET name — **2.10**, not 2.9 |
| F6 | `println("${tok}")` | String text, no interp — **5.8**, not a hide of IDENT |

Z2 is F1/F2. F3 is backend wiring (named HIGH residual). F4 is s71 R2
(named). F5/F6 are other sprint IDs.

2.9 unify would replace rail (1) with something equivalent to rail (3):
either depth-aware token walk, or lowered-args `c_secret_emit_guard`.
Until that lands, F1/F2 stay live on every path that has **only** rail (1).

