## emit-bc (HIGH residual — not the Z2 class)

`bc_emit*` does **not** call `check_secret_println_sinks`. Live:

| Probe | emit-bc | Bytes |
|-------|---------|-------|
| 01 `println(tok)` | **rc=0** | `PUSH_STR s3cret` / `CALL println 1` |
| 06 `println((n), tok)` | **rc=0** | `LOAD_LOCAL 1` / `LOAD_LOCAL 0` / `CALL println 2` |
| 14 wrapper | **rc=0** | `leak`: `CALL println 1`; `main`: `CALL leak 1` |

SECRET **reaches** the BC `println` opcode. This is fail-open of a
product backend, **orthogonal** to 2.9 unify (the walker is never
invoked). Do **not** soft-pass. Do **not** call it 2.8. Wire the
same front-door or refuse emit-bc as residual-unsealed.

---

## Root-cause (why Z1/Z3/Z5 hold and Z2 does not)

1. **Z1** — simple arg lists have no inner `RPAREN` before the SECRET
   IDENT. Front-door + stmt belt + (llvm) front-door all see it.
   `eprintln` free-call also hits `c_secret_emit_guard`.
2. **Z2** — front-door walk is a **flat** RPAREN stop. LLVM and wasm
   have **no second rail**. Stmt `c_emit_println` **does** continue
   after `c_emit_expr_c`, so emit-c still BLOCK. That is why Z5 PASS
   does not save Z2.
3. **Z3** — STRING is not IDENT; `c_parse_secret_env` is line-start
   only. Walker never treats string text as a name.
4. **Unify is not landed.** `c_emit_ident_stmt` still early-returns
   to `c_emit_println`. L175–193 is still the token loop, not
   `c_secret_emit_guard(env, id, …)`. DE2.9 SPRINT “What got fixed”
   row is the **check token refuse**, not this backlog close
   (hygiene agrees).

